The issues:

1. `title` is `&str` (borrowed), but `connect_activate` requires `'static`. I need to convert `title` and `app_id` to owned `String`s before the closure.

2. `connect_activate` requires `Fn` (not `FnOnce`), so `move` closure that moves `pump` and `on_event` — the closure is `Fn`, so moving is fine for `move` closures actually. But the error "cannot move out of on_event, a captured variable in an Fn closure" is confusing. Let me think.

Actually, `connect_activate` takes `F: Fn(&Application) + 'static`. A `move` closure can be `Fn` if it doesn't mutate/consume its captures when called multiple times. But `move || { ... on_event.borrow_mut()(ev, p) ... }` — the closure calls `on_event.borrow_mut()(...)`. `on_event` is `Rc<RefCell<F>>`. `.borrow_mut()` gives `RefMut<F>`, and calling it consumes... no.

Wait, the error at line 326 says "cannot move out of `on_event`". The `on_event` here (line 326) shadows the parameter. Line 326: `let on_event = Rc::new(RefCell::new(on_event));` — this moves the parameter `on_event` into the Rc. That's fine.

Then line 328: inside the idle closure `on_event.borrow_mut()(ev, p)`. `on_event` is `Rc<RefCell<F>>`. `borrow_mut()` → `RefMut<F>`. Calling `RefMut<F>` as a function: `RefMut` implements `DerefMut<Target=F>` and `Fn`-calling auto-derefs. But the compiler might complain because `RefMut<F>` needs `F: FnMut`, and calling a `RefMut` requires moving out of the `DerefMut`... Actually `(*refmut)(ev, p)` works if `F: FnMut`. But the error "cannot move out of on_event" suggests something else.

Hmm wait, the errors reference line 326 and 327 with "captured variable in an Fn closure". Let me re-read the exact errors:

```
error[E0507]: cannot move out of `on_event`, a captured variable in an `Fn` closure
   --> crates/pathland-gtk/src/lib.rs:326:45
   --> .../gio-.../application.rs:451:28
error[E0507]: cannot move out of `pump`, a captured variable in an `Fn` closure
   --> crates/pathland-gtk/src/lib.rs:327:30
```

Line 326 col 45: `let on_event = Rc::new(RefCell::new(on_event));` — col 45 is `on_event` (the parameter). This moves `on_event` (the parameter, an `F`). But this is inside the `connect_activate` closure (which is `Fn`). The `connect_activate` closure is `move |app| { ... }`. The closure captures `on_event` (the F parameter) by move. But the closure is `Fn` (connect_activate requires Fn). An `Fn` closure can't move out of its captured variables when called multiple times. Since `connect_activate`'s callback is `Fn` (may be called multiple times?), the closure body can't consume its captures.

So the problem: `connect_activate` requires the closure to be `Fn`, meaning it can be called multiple times, so the body can't move out of `on_event`/`pump`.

But `connect_activate` is semantically called once. gtk-rs types it as `Fn` though. The common workaround: wrap in `Rc<RefCell<Option<F>>>` and `.take()`, OR restructure so the closure doesn't move out of captured values.

The standard pattern in the existing gtk-demo was:
```rust
let demo = Rc::new(RefCell::new(Demo { ... }));
app.connect_activate(build_ui);  // actually it used connect_activate(build_ui) with a fn pointer
```

Actually the existing demo used `app.connect_activate(build_ui)` where `build_ui` is a plain function (fn pointer, which is Fn and Copy). And inside build_ui it created its own Rc. So the existing demo avoided capturing anything in connect_activate.

For my case, I capture `pump` (Rc<RefCell<P>>) and `on_event` (F) and `title`/`app_id` (borrowed). The `move` closure tries to move them in. Since the closure is `Fn`, moving `on_event` (a non-Copy F) out at line 326 fails.

Solution: 
1. Convert `app_id` and `title` to owned `String` before the closure.
2. Wrap `on_event` in `Rc<RefCell<Option<F>>>` before the closure, or `Rc<RefCell<F>>` directly and don't re-wrap inside.
3. Move `pump` (Rc is Clone, so I can clone it rather than move).

Let me restructure:

```rust
pub fn run_with_pump<P, F>(app_id: &str, title: &str, pump: Rc<RefCell<P>>, on_event: F)
where P: Pump + 'static, F: FnMut(Event, Rc<RefCell<P>>) + 'static
{
    let app_id = app_id.to_string();
    let title = title.to_string();
    let on_event = Rc::new(RefCell::new(on_event));
    let app = gtk::Application::builder().application_id(&app_id).build();

    app.connect_activate(move |app| {
        let window = gtk::ApplicationWindow::builder()
            .application(app)
            .title(&title)
            ...
        let renderer = Rc::new(RefCell::new(GtkRenderer::new()));
        // buttons sink: clone pump
        { let pump_for_buttons = pump.clone(); ... }
        pump_once(&renderer, &pump, &mut |_, _| {});
        ...
        glib::idle_add_local(move || {
            pump_once(&renderer, &pump, &mut |ev, p| {
                on_event.borrow_mut()(ev, p);
            });
            glib::ControlFlow::Continue
        });
    });
    app.run();
}
```

Now `pump` (Rc) is captured by the closure — but the closure is `Fn`, and `pump` is `Rc<RefCell<P>>` which is Clone. The closure body uses `pump.clone()` and `&pump` — it does NOT move `pump` out. So capturing `pump` by move into an Fn closure is fine (it moves the Rc in, but the Rc is used immutably/cloned inside).

Wait, but `move` closure captures `pump` by moving it INTO the closure. Then inside, `pump.clone()` (line 308) clones. And `&pump` (line 318, 328) borrows. And the inner idle closure (line 327) is another `move` closure that moves `renderer` and `pump` and `on_event` into it. But the outer closure is `Fn` — if the outer closure moves `pump` into the inner idle closure (line 327 `move ||` captures `pump`), that's moving `pump` out of the outer closure's captured variable, which violates `Fn`.

So the outer closure can't move `pump` into the inner idle closure. Instead, the inner idle closure should CLONE `pump` (Rc clone), not move it.

Line 327: `glib::idle_add_local(move || { pump_once(&renderer, &pump, ...) })` — the inner `move` closure moves `pump` (the Rc) and `renderer` (Rc) and `on_event` (Rc). But these are all Rc (Clone). The inner `move` closure should capture clones. Since `pump`, `renderer`, `on_event` are Rc, and the inner closure is `move`, it moves them in (moves the Rc handles). But that moves them OUT of the outer closure's captures → violates outer Fn.

Fix: inside the inner `move` closure, clone the Rcs first, then move the clones:
```rust
let pump2 = pump.clone();
let renderer2 = renderer.clone();
let on_event2 = on_event.clone();
glib::idle_add_local(move || {
    pump_once(&renderer2, &pump2, &mut |ev, p| { on_event2.borrow_mut()(ev, p); });
    glib::ControlFlow::Continue
});
```

This way the outer closure only clones (doesn't move out), and the inner closure moves the clones.

Also line 308 `let pump_for_buttons = pump.clone();` — this clones, fine.

Line 318 `pump_once(&renderer, &pump, ...)` — borrows, fine.

So the fixes:
1. `app_id`/`title` to owned String.
2. Wrap `on_event` in Rc<RefCell<F>> BEFORE the closure (outside), and use it inside without re-moving.
3. Clone Rcs before the inner idle closure.

3. **E0716 temporary value dropped**: `let batch = pump.borrow_mut().next_frame();` — `pump.borrow_mut()` creates a temporary `RefMut`, and `next_frame()` returns `OpcodeBatch<'_>` borrowing from the RefMut temporary. The temporary is dropped at the end of the statement, but `batch` (borrowing from it) is used after. Fix: bind the RefMut to a variable:
```rust
let mut pump_ref = pump.borrow_mut();
let batch = pump_ref.next_frame();
```
But then `renderer.borrow_mut()` inside the loop borrows renderer (different object), fine. But `pump_ref` holds the borrow of `pump` while `apply_frame` is called — that's fine (different objects). But next loop iteration re-borrows pump — `pump_ref` must be dropped first. Since `batch` borrows `pump_ref`, and `batch.frame()` is used inside, then `batch` drops at end of iteration. But `pump_ref` is declared outside the loop... Actually I'll restructure to do the borrow inside the loop iteration scope.

