Found it. `ApplicationExtManual::run()` does:
```rust
fn run(&self) -> ExitCode {
    self.run_with_args(&std::env::args().collect::<Vec<_>>())
}
```

So `app.run()` passes `std::env::args()` (the REAL process args = JVM's `-cp`, `-Djna.library.path`, etc.) to `g_application_run`, which parses them → "Unknown option -cp".

The fix: use `app.run_with_args(&[] as &[&str])` (empty args) instead of `app.run()`.

But there's also `gtk::init()` (called during Application::new) which calls `gtk_init_check()` with no args. Does `gtk_init_check()` also parse the real args? Let me check the GLib/GTK behavior.

Actually, the `gtk_init_check()` no-arg in GTK4 C is:
```c
gboolean
gtk_init_check (void)
{
  return gtk_init_check_abi (NULL, NULL);
}
```
Wait, actually the C signature `gtk_init_check()` has no parameters (it's `gtk_init_check(void)`). It internally calls `gtk_init_check(&argc, &argv)` with the REAL argc/argv from the platform? No...

Actually, in GTK4, `gtk_init_check(void)` is defined as a convenience that calls `gtk_init_check_abi(NULL, NULL)`. And `gtk_init_check_abi(int *argc, char ***argv)` calls `gtk_parse_args(argc, argv)`. When argc/argv are NULL, `gtk_parse_args` uses `g_get_prgname` and does NOT parse process args... or does it?

Actually, looking at GTK4 `gtk/gtkmain.c`:
```c
gboolean
gtk_init_check (void)
{
  return gtk_init_check_abi (NULL, NULL);
}

gboolean
gtk_init_check_abi (int *argc, char ***argv)
{
  if (!gtk_parse_args (argc, argv))
    return FALSE;
